            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for sleepless, then the Latin for to lie down."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: stertō, stertere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "stertō, stertere — \"to snore\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hospes ēbrius tōtam noctem stertit. The drunken guest snores all night."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "stertō, stertere, stertuī. English stertorous 'heavy and snoring' comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The origin of stertō is not settled: Lewis & Short compare Greek darthanō 'to sleep', but EtymDB gives no parent. English stertor and stertorous come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
