        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: obstrepō, obstrepere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "obstrepō, obstrepere — \"to make a din, to drown out with noise\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Virī ēbriī in caupōnā obstrepunt, et hospitēs queruntur. Drunken men make a din in the inn, and the guests complain."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "obstrepō, obstrepere, obstrepuī, obstrepitum, often with a dative: to roar at someone. English obstreperous comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "obstrepō is built from ob- 'against' and strepō 'to make a noise' (Lewis & Short). English obstreperous comes from obstreperus, built on it."
            }
          ]
        },
